data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Transfer Failure**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on defining *transfer failure*. I've clarified that the risk remains high even when adding the candidate to a detector family. This is due to representation insufficiency, violating security assumptions. I recognize the relative nature of this failure, based on the defense/representation space. I'm focusing on defining it not in absolute terms, but relative to the defense used. I'm also planning to focus on the historical context, such as timing attacks, which have broken existing security models.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1714,"totalTokenCount": 3072,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1714}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1358,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "j-gCa97DNfejuMYP_OiYuAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Identifying New Failures**\n\nI'm now identifying examples that reveal fundamental breaches. I see Return-Oriented Programming bypassing W⊕X, Rowhammer and Spectre/Meltdown shattering memory isolation, and adversarial examples showing ML model vulnerabilities. These demonstrate how existing defenses are insufficient. My current focus is on understanding what characterizes a fundamental failure.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1714,"totalTokenCount": 3329,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1714}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1615,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "j-gCa97DNfejuMYP_OiYuAs"}

