data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Rarity Definition**\n\nI'm now focusing on a plan to determine, without an LLM judge, the rareness of a hypothesis within a formal grammar, denoted as $\\mathcal{G}$. I plan to canonize hypotheses, h, into equivalence classes, [$h$]. I'll then calculate the empirical frequency of proposing each hypothesis, denoted as $\\hat{p}(h)$, on a budget of independent draws. The surprisal, $-\\log \\hat{p}(h)$, will then be estimated.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1714,"totalTokenCount": 18592,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1714}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 16878,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "j-gCa97DNfejuMYP_OiYuAs"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Rarity Measurement**\n\nI'm now integrating methods for measuring hypothesis rarity. For frequentist metrics, I'm examining Shannon entropy $H(\\hat{p})$, Hill numbers, and Chao1 coverage/richness. For structural approaches, the edit distance of the ancestor tree (AST) helps. I'm focusing on measurable validation on independent data with FDR or FWER control, and on estimating predictive loss or log-likelihood. I'm prioritizing the marginal predictive gain $\\Delta L(h)$ for downstream utility.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1714,"totalTokenCount": 18906,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1714}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 17192,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-neon","responseId": "j-gCa97DNfejuMYP_OiYuAs"}

